\name{stats_meta}
\alias{stats_meta}
\title{Pool one effect size per study into a random-effects estimate per feature}
\usage{
stats_meta(studies, confidence = 0.95, timeout = 60)
}
\arguments{
\item{studies}{A data.frame with one row per study and columns \code{feature}, \code{estimate} and
  \code{standard_error}. A feature's studies need not be adjacent. The standard error must be
  positive.}

\item{confidence}{Coverage of the reported interval, as a fraction (0 to 1).}

\item{timeout}{Seconds to allow, or \code{NULL} to wait indefinitely.
}

}
\description{
Pool one effect size per study into a random-effects estimate per feature (DerSimonian-Laird), with heterogeneity, direction agreement and the largest leave-one-out change.

Each feature is pooled on its own by mzLib's \code{RandomEffectsMeta.Pool}, with the
DerSimonian-Laird (1986) moment estimator of the between-study variance, as metafor's
\code{rma(method = "DL")} does (mzLib matches metafor to 1e-8 relative). Every row also says how many
studies agree in direction and how far the estimate moves when any one is dropped - the two
checks a reader of a pooled result asks for first.
}
\value{
An \code{mzlibr_meta_analysis}: \code{records}, a data.frame with one row per feature in order of
  first appearance - \code{feature}; \code{studies} pooled; \code{estimate}, \code{standard_error}, \code{confidence_low}
  and \code{confidence_high} (units of the study estimates); \code{p_value} (a fraction, 0 to 1, normal
  reference, not adjusted across features); \code{tau2} (squared units of the study estimates); \code{q}
  (a chi-square statistic) and \code{i_squared} (a fraction, 0 to 1), both \code{NA} for one study;
  \code{direction_agree} and \code{direction_disagree} (studies); and \code{leave_one_out_max_delta} (units of
  the study estimates, \code{NA} for one study). Also \code{study_count} studies, \code{feature_count}
  features, \code{confidence} (a fraction, 0 to 1), \code{row_count} rows and \code{caveats}.

}
\section{With few studies}{


DerSimonian-Laird with a normal reference: the interval and p-value do not widen for few studies
(no Hartung-Knapp adjustment), so treat a pooled estimate from two or three studies with care.
Send \code{records$p_value} to \code{\link{stats_adjust}} to adjust across features.

}
